The current version falls back to a linear scan of every message for any number at or above 1024. Manufacturer-specific numbers in 0xFF00–0xFFFE never match, so each one costs a full scan.

This rewrite indexes all 65536 possible message numbers, so every lookup is a direct table access with no scan. Each entry is a 16-bit index (128 KB in total) rather than a pointer, which would need 512 KB on 64-bit, so the table stays more cache-friendly.

The table is filled from the last message down to the first, so when two messages share a number the first one wins, as in the original scan. `MESGS` is far smaller than 65535, so 0xFFFF is safe to use as the "not found" value.

```cpp
const Profile::MESG* Profile::GetMesg(const FIT_UINT16 num)
{
    struct Index
    {
        FIT_UINT16 idx[65536];
        Index()
        {
            for (int k = 0; k < 65536; k++)
                idx[k] = 0xFFFF;
            for (int i = MESGS - 1; i >= 0; i--)
                idx[(FIT_UINT16)mesgs[i].num] = (FIT_UINT16)i;
        }
    };
    static const Index table;

    const FIT_UINT16 i = table.idx[num];
    if (i == 0xFFFF)
        return NULL;
    return &mesgs[i];
}
```